      "model": "gpt-6.1-sol",
      "question": "I've heard the ratio method can give odd answers when gas levels are low, so I want a second opinion on Transformer 1 at site MAIN. Using the average hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene over the available window, run the IEC 60599 ratio classification. Separately, work out the Duval Triangle 1 percentages from methane, ethylene and acetylene, and name the zone they fall in (PD, D1, D2, DT, T1, T2 or T3). Tell me whether the two methods agree. If they disagree, tell me which one you trust more and why, looking at whether the gases are high enough for ratios to mean anything. Then name the single most likely failure mode from our known transformer list. Use averages, not single samples. If the combined methane, ethylene and acetylene is under 10 ppm, say that the Duval result isn't meaningful and rely on the ratio result alone. If methane or ethylene has no data at all, say that neither method can be applied reliably and give a key-gas view from the largest gas instead.",
      "answer": "### Transformer 1 \u2014 MAIN\nUsing all **5 samples, January 15\u201319, 2024**, the average concentrations are:\n\n| Gas | Average (ppm) |\n|---|---:|\n| Hydrogen | 2,869.0 |\n| Methane | 7,331.2 |\n| Ethane | 5,457.4 |\n| Ethylene | 16,626.8 |\n| Acetylene | 7.0 |\n\n**IEC 60599 ratios** (calculated directly because the interpretation tool was unavailable):\n- C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 = **0.000421**\n- CH\u2084/H\u2082 = **2.555**\n- C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 = **3.047**\n\nThese meet **T3: thermal fault above 700\u00b0C** criteria: respectively <0.2, >1, and >3.\n\n**Duval Triangle 1**, normalizing each gas by CH\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2084 + C\u2082H\u2082:\n- Methane: **30.591%**\n- Ethylene: **69.380%**\n- Acetylene: **0.029%**\n- Zone: **T3**\n\n**Agreement:** Both methods indicate **T3**. The combined Duval gases are **23,965 ppm**, comfortably above your 10-ppm cutoff. Hydrogen and the thermal hydrocarbons are also abundant, so this is not a low-gas ratio artefact. The IEC ethylene/ethane ratio is close to its T2/T3 boundary, but Duval independently supports T3.\n\n**Single most likely known failure mode:** **Thermal Fault \u2014 Core/Winding**. This is a diagnostic indication, not confirmation of the fault\u2019s physical location.",
