          "generalized_result_verification": false,
          "agent_sequence_correct": false,
          "clarity_and_justification": false,
          "hallucinations": true,
          "suggestions": "The agent correctly retrieved mean gas values via iot.sensor_stats for Transformer 1 at MAIN (window Jan 15-19, 2024) and computed the Duval Triangle 1 percentages correctly (30.6% CH4, 69.4% C2H4, 0.03% C2H2 -> T3, sum 23,965 ppm well above 10 ppm). However, it did not call fmsr.interpret_dga as expected; it merely asserted the tool was 'unavailable' with no visible failed attempt in the trajectory. More importantly, its manual IEC 60599 classification misstates the standard: the T3 criterion for C2H4/C2H6 is >4 (T2 is 1-4), not >3. With C2H4/C2H6 = 3.047 the IEC ratio result is T2 (300-700 C), not T3, so the two methods actually disagree and the agent should have said which it trusts more (Duval, given the very high absolute gas levels) rather than claiming agreement. The agent should retry/verify the fmsr.interpret_dga call, apply the correct IEC thresholds, explicitly report the T2-vs-T3 disagreement, and then justify the final failure mode (Thermal Fault - Core/Winding is a reasonable mapping for a high-temperature thermal fault)."
